["home/boiler-pressure-low-or-high","The Gauge And The Causes","Boiler pressure has one gauge, two failure directions and three causes per direction: low pressure is usually leaks or the expansion vessel, high pressure is the filling loop or vessel failure. The repressurise steps run safely and in order.","Read the gauge","The green band and what the needle's drift tells.","Low pressure causes","Slow leaks, vessel issues and radiator bleed history.","High pressure causes","Filling loops left open and failed vessels.","The repressurise steps","The filling loop procedure, in safe order.","The bleed sequence","Radiators first, boiler settings second.","When to call","Vessel failures and persistent drift need engineers.","The boiler gauge is the heating system's vital sign. Read it correctly and half the winter callouts never happen.","home"],
["home/sump-pump-failure-signs","The Ten-Second Test","Sump pumps fail quietly until the storm arrives: the ten-second test is a bucket pour that proves the float, the check valve and the discharge line together. The failure signs are listed in the order they appear.","The ten-second test","Bucket, pour, listen: the whole test in one pour.","The float","Sticking floats are the single most common failure.","The check valve","Backflow after shutdown means the valve has quit.","The discharge line","Frozen and blocked exits defeat perfect pumps.","Power backup","Battery and water backups, honestly compared.","The annual service","Clean, test and date: the three-step spring ritual.","A sump pump earns its keep in the storm it never sees. Run the monthly ten-second test and the quiet failures surface first.","home"],
